  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nBroad upper level troughing across the west with stronger \nembedded upper low over SW SK is resulting in mainly light snow \nacross much of the region. While the majority of forcing \ndiminishes through the morning today, lingering cyclonic \nnorthwesterly flow aloft will be enough to maintain some areas of \nlight snow through the day today across the region. Those with \ntravel plans early this morning should plan on slick roads, even \nif snow amounts are light. \n\nBrief, weak ridging moves in this evening, which will allow for \nat least brief clearing over many areas. Areas that see the \noverlap between an existing sizable snowpack, clearing skies, and \nlight winds tonight into Sunday morning will see one of the \ncoldest mornings of the winter so far. In addition to the cold \novernight temperatures, forecast soundings indicate fog will be a \nconcern heading into Sunday morning. \n\nAs quick as the weak ridging moves in this evening, it gets \nushered away by late Sunday morning ahead of a larger trough near \nthe Pacific NW coast. Several weaker waves eject from this \nweakening troughing toward the Northern Rockies Sunday into \nMonday. Given the overall weakening nature of the system and \nassociated waves, precipitation that falls near the Continental \nDivide late Sunday into Monday is looking light. \n\nAnother brief stretch of ridging builds in Tuesday into Tuesday \nnight, but quickly gets ushered eastward as a more progressive \nzonal flow develops thereafter toward Thanksgiving. Tuesday and \nmuch of Wednesday are looking dry and more mild at this point, but\nas the zonal flow develops toward Thanksgiving and Friday, a mix \nof rain and snow will sneak back into the forecast near the \nContinental Divide. Cluster guidance is nearly unanimous in an \nupper level ridge building in next weekend, though it struggles to\nresolve just how amplified it will be. Weaker ridges are a bit \ncooler and continue opportunities for rain/snow along the \nContinental Divide while stronger ridges trend drier and warmer. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThe main source of uncertainty in the near term is with respect \nto just how quickly snow subsides through the day today. Cyclonic \nnorthwest flow aloft is a good setup for persistent light snow, \nthough many forecast soundings dry the lower levels out quickly \nthis morning. Should the slower drying trend play out, light snow \nwould continue across many areas through the morning and early \nafternoon.\n\nThe other largest piece of uncertainty is for the development of \nfog tonight. Guidance suggests a light upslope flow will develop \nacross the plains tonight where skies look to at least briefly \nclear. Combine this with an existing snowpack and saturation (fog)\nwould seem quite reasonable. Forecast soundings are not \nparticularly excited about this prospect however (mainly due to \nlingering cloud cover), which leads to lower confidence in \nimpactful fog developing in most areas at this time horizon. -AM",
